Labour is no longer Liberal.
Don’t tolerate those who express an opinion
When Momentum activists voted for my reprimanders, it barely surprised me.
After all, I'm the last of a line of Labour MPs who they've said it wasn't welcome anymore - and all for expressing an opinion.
My colleague from parliament, Joan Ryan, received a similar treatment because she unresolutely opposed anti-Semitism.
In my case, the impetus for reprimanding criticized that I disagreed with Jeremy Corbyn.
It was the importance of accountable economic policy, about national security, about Europe, ironically about similar issues that Jeremy did not agree with the previous leaders.
At an invitation to the Labour Meeting for Nottingham on Friday, "we want meetings to be inclusive and productive."
For the majority of my eight years as a local Labour deputy, Friday evening GC sessions were exactly that.
Unfortunately, today many sessions do not carry in such a tone, and the promise of “mersicious, more subterrested” politics has long been forgotten, if it has ever paid.
It's increasingly clear that diverging views don't tolerate each other in the Labour Party, and every view is judged by whether it's acceptable to the party's leadership.
It started shortly after Jeremy came into the lead, because the colleagues I previously thought we shared a similar political stance suddenly were expected to turn 180 degrees and accept the views that I would otherwise have disagreed with - whether national security or the EU's unanimity.
Whenever I speak publicly – and it doesn’t really matter what I say – there’s a tirade of criticism on social media calling for an appeal, waivers the politics of the center, and listening to how I shouldn’t be in the Labour Party.
This isn't just my experience.
Of course, I know that I am more fortunate than some of my colleagues, because the comments directed at me tend to be only political.
Admilige the professionalism and dedication of colleagues who face thawing sexual or racist attacks on a daily basis.
One of the biggest disappointments of this political era is how the level of attacks has normalized.
Jeremy Corbyn claimed last week that the Labour Party should promote a culture of tolerance.
In fact, we are no longer the famous Liberals, and with every impetus to express “disconfidence” or a change of selection rules, the party is increasingly narrowing.
Over the past two years, a lot of people have advised me not to stand out too much from the crowd, be quiet and keep pace, and then I'll be "okay."
But I didn't go into politics for that.
Since, as a student 32 years ago, I joined the Labour Party, provocated by the indulgence of Thatcher's government, who committed that my high-rise class has literally collapsed, I've been trying to push for better utilities for those who needed it the most -- whether in the local council or at the ministry.
"I've never hidden my policy, not even at the last election.
No one at the Nottingham East constituency could be confused about my political stancies and dissolution areas.
To those who backed the complaint on Friday, I would only say that when a country sneaks towards Brexit, which hurts households, businesses and our public services, I do not understand the desire to grieve in time and energy to deal with my loyalty to the leader of the Labour Party.
But the main message I have doesn't belong to Momentum in Nottingham. It's for the residents of my perimeter, whether they're members of the Labour Party or not: I'm tempting that I've been able to serve you and I'm promising you that no threats of revocation or political compulsition won't scare me away from trying to pursue what I believe is in the best interests of all of you.
Chris Leslie's Speupplement for Nottingham East
