John Edward: Without language skills, a global citizen can’t
Scottish independent schools maintain excellent academic achievements and this trend continues in 2018 with an additional year of extraordinary exam results, further strengthening individual and colludentary achievements in sports, art, music and other social activities.
With more than 30,000 pupils throughout Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), strive to provide the highest level of service to their pupils and their parents.
Independent schools try to prepare pupils for further and higher education, careers according to their wishes and a place in the world as global citizens.
As an educating sector capable of designing and implementing tailor-made school curriculum, we still perceive modern languages as a popular and desirable selection item in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "When you speak to a man with languages that he or she understands, you speak to his head.
Speak to him with his own language, you speak to his heart.”
It's a fierce commemoration that if we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries, we can't rely solely on English.
From this year's rehearsal results, we'll see that the languages are at the top of a table with the highest average grades among independent schools.
In total, 68 per cent of pupils studying foreign languages achieved the highest A-mark.
Data collected from 74 SCIS member schools have shown that 72 per cent of students achieved the tallest A grade in Mandarin, 72 per cent in German, 69 per cent in french and 63 per cent in Spanish.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as the basic skills that children and young people will no doubt need in the future.
Languages today, as selective subjects in independent and other schools are as important as STEM (science, technology, engineering and mathe mathematics).
In 2014, the Commission's Employment and Skill Skills survey found that 17 per cent lack of language skills made up among the reasons that employers cited as job occupancy problems.
That’s why language skills are becoming an ever-increasing necessity in preparing young people for their future career.
As there are more and more prospective job opportunities that require languages, these capabilities in a globalised world are becoming fundamental.
Regardless of what career a man chooses, as long as he has learned a second language, with such a lifelong skill he will have a real advantage for the future.
Ability to communicate directly with people from foreign countries will automatically bring a multilingual person an advantage over the competition.
According to a YouGov survey among more than 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom in 2013, 75 per cent couldn't speak any foreign language enough to convene smoothly, with french French being the only language in which a number of people reached double-fair percentages (15 per cent).
That is why it is important for today's children to invest in language learning.
Knowledge of a number of languages, in particular the languages of developed-economy-developed countries, will give children a better chance to find a meaningful job.
In Scotia, each school will vary depending on what languages it teaches.
Some schools will focus on more classically modern languages, while others will teach languages that are most prominent for the United Kingdom in terms of the future in 2020, such as Mandarin Chinese or japanese.
No matter what your child is interested in, at independent schools he/she will always be able to choose from a number of languages and will be educated by experts in this field.
Scotland's independent schools are committed to providing an educational environment that prepares for children and armaments with the skills needed for success, no matter what the future holds.
At the moment, it is not possible to confuse the fact that in the global business environment languages remain fundamentally important for the future of the country, and this must be reflected in education.
Modern languages should indeed be called "international communicative capabilities."
Independent schools will continue to offer this opportunity, diversity and excellent quality for young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward's headteacher of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
