“Hargarten students take a course on how to relax enough
The goal of this year's new course at Harvard University this year is to ensure that all students have more sleep in an effort to shuffle the growing trend of studying all night with the help of Caffeine.
Scientists have found that students at the world's leading university are often helpless when it comes to the complete foundations of caring for your health.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at the Harvard Medical School and Specialist Brigham and Women's Clinic, drafted a course that he said was the first of its kind in the United States.
He was inspired to create the course after the lecture he led about sleep deprivation and its impact on learning.
“At the very end, a girl came to me and asked, ‘Why am I only seeing it now, in the last year?’
“She said no one had ever told her about the meaning of sleep, which surprised me,” he told The Telegraph.
For the first time this year, the course explained to students the basics about how good sleep habits help both academic and athletic performance, while improving general health.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at the Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's health service, said the university decided to open a course when she found that students were suffering from severe sleep deprivation during the working week.
An hour-long course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one part there is a picture of a dormitory room on the screen, where students can click on cups with coffee, curtains, sneakers and books, and learn about the effects of caffeine, light, and how sleep deprivation affects athletic performance and how important a regular sleep regime is.
Subsequently, attendees will learn how a long-term sleep deficit can increase the risk of heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
The map of the university campus with interactive icons further encourages participants to reflect on their daily routine.
"We don't know it won't change students' behaviour immediately.
But we're confident they'd have the right to know about it - just as everyone has the right to know the health effects of cigarette smoking," Professor Czeisler added.
There is still a culture in which students are proud to be able to learn all last night, he says, adding that modern technology and growing pressure on students means sleep deprivation has become a growing problem.
Ensuring sufficient sleep of good quality should be a "secret weapon" of students to combat stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he said -- even against gaining weight, because the sleep deficit gets the brain into starvation mode, so they constantly feel hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old caliliphonist's chemistry and biology, helped Professor Czeisler propose a course after attending one of his seminars last year during his first year at Harvard.
He said the course had opened his eyes and inspired him to pursue a course at the level of the entire university.
He hopes that in the next step it will be possible to ask all post-graduate students to complete a similar study program before embarking on a difficult study.
Czeisler advises students to set an alarm clock at a time when they are supposed to go to bed, as well as a time when to wake up and realize the harmful effects of “blue light” that eradicate electronic screens and LED lighting, which can disrupt your circadian rhythm, which leads to falling-out problems.
