National Trust uncovers the secret life of bats
The new research, which takes place on the estate in the Scottish Highlands, aims to unleash how bats use the landscape in their food.
We hope that the findings will shed a new light on the behavior of unique flying mammals and will help guide future nature conservation activities.
A study by scientists from the National Trust for Scotland will be followed by bats whitish and bat whistling, as well as the ears of bright and water bats in Inverewe gardens in the Wester Ross area.
Special loggers will be placed in key locations around the property to monitor the activities of bats throughout the season.
Employees of this organization and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using manual detectors.
Professional sound analysis of all recordings determines the frequency of calls of bats and the activities of individual species.
They will then create a map of their natural environment and a report to create a detailed picture of their behavior on a large scale.
Rob Dewar, an adviser for nature conservation for NTS, hopes that the results will unreveal which areas of the natural environment are the most important for bats and how they are used by individual species.
This information will help determine the benefits of environmental management work, such as meadow-creating and how best to preserve forests for bats and other related species.
Non-pair populations in Scotland and the United Kingdom have declined considerably over the past century.
They are at risk of construction and development work, which have an impact on grassland and environmental loss.
Wind turbines and illumination, as well as planes and some chemical modifications of building materials, as well as domestic cat attacks, may also be a danger.
Bats aren't actually blind.
However, due to their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are like the eyes when it comes to hunting for prey.
They take advantage of the sophisticated echococation technique to accurately identify insects and obstacles in their flight path.
NTS, responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 acres of land across the country, takes bats very seriously.
He has ten trained experts who regularly carry out surveys, survey inspections and sometimes rescues.
The organization has even established the first and only reserved protected area for bats on the Threave estate in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight out of the ten types of bats in Scotland.
Real estate manager David Thompson, says the dormitory is an immaculate territory for them.
"Here in Threava we have an amazing space for bats," he said.
"We've got old buildings, lots of sheltered trees and all good habitats.
"But there's still so much we don't know about bats, so the work we're doing here and on other real estate will help us better understand what they need to survive."
Emphasizes that it is important to insucate the bats before carrying out maintenance within the property, as it's possible that the unconscious destruction of one maternity gathering could kill up to 400 females and chicks, possibly destroying an entire local population.
Non-pairs are protected and it is illegal to kill, torture, disturb them, or destroy their congregations.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish clerk for Bat Conservation Trust, has encouraged the public to be involved.
She said: "We still have a lot to learn about our bats, and we just don't really know about many of our species how their populations live."
